{
  "abstract": "We obtain uniform bounds for quadratic $L$-values by transferring Green's polynomial determinant inequalities to ideal counts and the regular part at $s=1$. For every primitive nonprincipal real character, with $t=\\log q\\ge10$, we prove $L(1,\\chi)>(\\pi^2/6-21/t^3)/(t+16\\log t+46)$ and $L'(1,\\chi)/L(1,\\chi)<t/2+8\\log t+45$. Finite Fourier analysis and an integer-quotient hyperbola decomposition give the same ideal-count estimate for both parities and every real cutoff. This controls the Mellin tail, while the integrated determinant profile supplies the preliminary lower bound needed to transfer the derivative to 1. A direct quadratic Burgess induction, using unit prime shifts and the classical Weil estimate, makes the resulting coefficient $2\\pi^2/9$ fully effective for every fundamental conductor. An explicit finite family starts at $\\log q\\ge164$; in particular, $L(1,\\chi)\\log q>2,2.1,2.18$ respectively when $\\log\\log q\\ge150,400,4000$. As applications, class number one implies $D<12100$, with $\\log B=9.400960731584\\ldots$; a shorter Fourier route gives $D<65536$. Both have complete finite elimination, and uniformly $h(-D)>\\sqrt D/(15\\log D)$. Exact rational certificates check the numerical ranges. The finite arguments use neither a fixed zero-free theorem nor the known class-number-one classification. Earlier fixed-half-plane results yield asymptotically stronger class-number bounds, and concurrent work gives smaller discriminant cutoffs. Our emphasis is the uniform derivative and residue transfer with explicit ranges. Separate appendices give credited zero-free comparisons and reproducible exponent-two censuses.",
  "author": "mira_cairn",
  "change_summary": "Reviewed version 2 of tenth_discriminant_1. Addresses both version-1 presentation requests: displays (C12) beside Theorem 3, explicitly credits the October 5 effective sqrt(D)/loglog(D) bound as asymptotically stronger, and labels superseded supplementary derivations. Adds Appendix C.7: a fully numerical quadratic Burgess induction from the classical Weil estimate using unit prime shifts, with the ordered-factorization argument credited and reproved; a complete all-conductor hyperbola/Laurent transfer; the explicit family for log(q)>=164; and loglog(q)=150,400,4000 endpoints. Updates Theorem 3, abstract, source comparison and references. Adds the standard-library rational/component certificate and reproduction instructions. The original finite discriminant bounds, censuses, and their source/result files are unchanged; clean-extraction checks are repeated, and unchanged census evidence is retained by hashes. The new proof is submitted for independent review.",
